<header class="ref-head yr-head"><p class="cty-kicker">Year by year · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p><h1 class="research-title">1877</h1><p class="ref-lede">1877 in world history: 27 events, 21 births, 21 deaths, 1 work and 1 state that came to an end. 950 recorded states existed that year.</p></header>
<nav class="ref-letters yr-near" aria-label="Nearby years"><a href="/years/1873">1873</a><a href="/years/1874">1874</a><a href="/years/1875">1875</a><a href="/years/1876">1876</a><a href="/years/1877" aria-current="page">1877</a><a href="/years/1878">1878</a><a href="/years/1879">1879</a><a href="/years/1880">1880</a><a href="/years/1881">1881</a></nav>
<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/british-raj.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-1">1 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Queen Victoria proclaimed Empress of India at the Delhi Durbar</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">At a vast imperial assembly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, Viceroy Lord Lytton proclaimed Victoria Empress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The new title, devised by Disraeli, marked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a>'s claim to succeed the Mughal emperors.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-2">2 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Rutherford B. Hayes declared winner of the disputed 1876 election</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Congress confirmed the Electoral Commission's award of all disputed votes to Hayes, giving him victory by one electoral vote. Under the Compromise of 1877, federal troops left the South.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/moscow" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moscow.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-4">4 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Tchaikovsky's 'Swan Lake' premieres at the Bolshoi</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The first production in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> was poorly received. Revived in St Petersburg in 1895 with choreography by Petipa and Ivanov, it became the most famous of all ballets.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-24">24 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Russia declares war on the Ottoman Empire</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> went to war in support of the Balkan Christians. The peace that followed made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> fully independent and created an autonomous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-9">9 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The first Wimbledon Championships begin</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The All <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> Croquet and Lawn Tennis Club opened its first lawn tennis championship, with 22 men competing. Spencer Gore won the title ten days later, launching the world's oldest tennis tournament.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-9">9 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The Nez Perce and the US Army clash at the Battle of the Big Hole</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The battle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montana">Montana</a> Territory, fought over two days between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Army and the Nez Perce during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nez-perce-war">Nez Perce War</a>, cost both sides heavy casualties. The Nez Perce withdrew in good order and went on with their long fighting retreat toward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nez-perce-war">Nez Perce War</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/september-5">5 September</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Crazy Horse is killed at Camp Robinson</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Oglala Lakota leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crazy-horse">Crazy Horse</a>, a victor at Little Bighorn, was bayoneted by a soldier while resisting arrest at Camp Robinson, Nebraska. He died that night.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/saigo-takamori" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saigo-takamori.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/september-24">24 September</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Saigō Takamori's samurai fall at the Battle of Shiroyama</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima">Kagoshima</a>, the heavily outnumbered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saigo-takamori">Saigō Takamori</a> made a last stand against Imperial Japanese Army troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamagata-aritomo">Yamagata Aritomo</a> and were annihilated. The battle ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-rebellion">Satsuma Rebellion</a>, the last internal mutiny in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saigo-takamori">Saigō Takamori</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamagata-aritomo">Yamagata Aritomo</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-rebellion">Satsuma Rebellion</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima">Kagoshima</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-21">21 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Edison announces his phonograph</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-edison">Thomas Edison</a> announced his invention of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phonograph">phonograph</a>, a machine that recorded sound on tinfoil and played it back. It was the first device able to reproduce the human voice.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/december-6">6 December</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Edison records and plays back 'Mary Had a Little Lamb'</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-edison">Thomas Edison</a> spoke the nursery rhyme into his tinfoil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phonograph">phonograph</a> and heard his voice played back. It was the first time a machine had reproduced recorded human speech.</p></div></li></ol></section>
